menu "PEX native modules"

config PEX
    bool "PEX loader (native modules from LittleFS)"
    default y
    help
      The PEX runtime (pex/runtime, docs/pex-format.md): load .pex modules
      from LittleFS into RAM and run them -- apps on the main loop, native Lua
      modules, ops for the web UI. It costs no RAM until a module is first
      loaded: the pool is taken from the C heap then, after a headroom check.
      Nothing runs at boot unless the launch list says so.

config PEX_POOL_KB
    int "PEX pool, static (KB; 0 = take a small one from the C heap)"
    depends on PEX
    default 0
    help
      Module RW, the modules' long-term memory, scratch arenas and pools,
      and the code of modules loaded into RAM (spec 7.2). A static block of
      its own: apart from the Lua arena (a Lua VM restart never touches it)
      and from the C heap. The shipped configs take it out of the Lua
      arena's budget (CONFIG_LUA_HEAP_KB) now that native modules do the
      work and Lua is a launcher; installed modules (XIP) need only their
      RW here.

      0 keeps the v1 behaviour: a pool of up to PEX_HEAP_POOL_KB taken from
      the C heap on first use.

config PEX_HEAP_POOL_KB
    int "PEX pool from the C heap (KB)"
    depends on PEX && PEX_POOL_KB = 0
    default 12
    help
      Only without a static pool: taken on first use from the C heap, as
      much as this but never leaving less than PEX_HEAP_RESERVE_KB free (the
      SD build has ~19 KB of C heap once booted, and pico_malloc panics when
      it runs out), and at least 6 KB.

config PEX_HEAP_RESERVE_KB
    int "C heap kept free when the pool is taken (KB)"
    depends on PEX && PEX_POOL_KB = 0
    default 8
    help
      pico_malloc panics on ANY failed allocation, so the rest of the
      firmware keeps this much. The pool is taken with newlib's own malloc
      (no panic) and shrinks to fit, down to 6 KB.

config PEX_XIP
    bool "Install modules into the PEX flash partition and run them in place"
    depends on PEX
    default y
    help
      pex.install writes a module into the PEX partition (fs.h FS_PEX_OFF,
      256 KB at 0x3BC000 on the 4 MB part), with an index kept power-fail
      safe (two copies, sequence numbers); an installed module loads with
      its code in flash (executed through QMI ATRANS1 on RP2350) and only
      its RW in the pool. pex.load prefers the installed copy.

config PEX_ALLOW_UNSIGNED
    bool "Load unsigned modules (development)"
    depends on PEX
    default y if !OTA
    default n
    help
      Off: only modules signed with the OTA key (tools/ota_sign.py, pexlink
      --sign) load. On: unsigned ones too, and pex.list says so. A build
      without OTA has no key to check signatures with, so it defaults on.

config PEX_LUA
    bool "Lua launcher and require for PEX modules"
    depends on PEX && LUA_ENABLE
    default y

config PEX_SLICE_MS
    int "Abort a PEX call that runs longer than this (ms)"
    depends on PEX
    range 0 4000
    default 500
    help
      Every module shares the main loop. A call into a module that does not
      return within this many milliseconds is stopped: that module is marked
      faulted, its tasks are cancelled, the event is recorded in
      var/pex_faults.json, and the loop continues with the other modules and
      the rest of the firmware. 0 leaves only the watchdog, which resets the
      whole board. The watchdog still does that if the module runs with
      interrupts masked, because the slice is a timer interrupt.

endmenu
